resistance  um so there's kind of a breakdown here  the number of different genes and snips  that we've identified that  aries genetics has identified that we've  included in this database for you to  investigate further  on the algorithm side um  [Music]  for amr prediction we have a find  resistance uh nucleotide database tool  the uh find resistance with point finder  and  find resistance with shortbread tools  and these have  different inputs and different outputs  depending on the type of data that you  you're working with and types of  analysis that you need  define resistance with nucleotide  database tool requires context  or whole genomes as input and it'll  identify genes  conferring resistance to really any gene  database that you provide but it's  it's intended to be used with one of the  databases that we provide is download  but  you of course can create your own as  well the fine resistance with point  finder  uh is run on raw data and it then  compares that to  a very highly well curated database of  point mutations that's maintained by dtu  in denmark  and then the output of that is a table  of resistance markers and specific snips  that are associated with with resistance  the shortbread tool which anecdotally is  one of my favorites also runs on raw  reads  and this uses what's called a short red  database which is a database of  pre-compiled protein markers  that are designed these are  antibiotic resistance gene or proteins  and the specific markers associated with  resistance that are  not found in uh any other genes  other than these resistance genes and so  you run your raw reeds  through the shortbread pipeline and it  will take those reads out  that have a high level of similarity or  would be predicted  to have a high level of similarity to  dna that would encode these specific  protein markers so it's a way of using  protein data  on protein markers to actually query  your genomic data  um so especially for hypothesis  generation and the ability to identify  novel uh antibiotic resistance markers  shortbread i think does an excellent job  at this because of the  um sort of inherent flexibility of um  dna sequence as it as it gets translated  into into protein sequence right so  it's a very broad range there and it's  it's a great way to identify new markers  that  might not otherwise be present in the  databases themselves  so here's a simple workflow where your  raw data is coming in  we do saw a trimming cell up front and  then after trimming the reeds we run  those raw reads through shortbread  and through point finder we then do a  denoble assembly on those reeds  and then after the assembly is completed  we run those assemblies through the fine  resistance with nucleotide database  so this is a simple workflow that takes  raw fsq files as input  and the output is a analysis using three  different antibiotic resistance  methods as to whether or not your  isolate actually has resistance genes  and specifically where they are  and what the nature is this is a  snapshot of  uh what some of the outputs might look  like in clc  um in the upper left-hand corner you  have the output of our  gene finding tool where in this case  there were five different genes that  were identified  their level of similarity to what was in  the database um the length of the match  and so forth the specific context that  they were identified on etc  um in the upper right hand corner uh  these are reeds that were  i you could say word will bend in other  words they had a high level of  similarity to  known antibiotic resistance genes um  that were pulled out using the point  finder tool um  the lower left corner uh this is a list  of um  potential drug resistance phenotypes  that were identified in your sample  um and uh the  number of um uh hits  that were assigned to each one of those  now a note here for those who are  sort of eagle-eyed you'll notice that a  lot of these sort of generically say  antibiotic molecule  um and this is because the underlying  phenotypic  uh ontology that this actually  phenotypic aro number that's assigned to  these  um in the card database that has uh  identical  names so this is a known issue that  we're addressing in a next  a new release in the near future that  will have a better resolution here on  the actual uh title here so don't be uh  don't be scared off now but if you were  to click through  and um uh follow these links to your  uh with your web browser you would see  indeed that um  what the specific antibiotic resistance  markers were and what the drugs were  that were associated with those  so bringing this all together you have a  single workflow here that does  raw fast q files up front as input  we trim the reeds we do a denotable  assembly on those reeds  and we can find antibiotic resistance  genes and also do our qc  the raw rates themselves um then can as  i mentioned earlier which can be run  into our point finder tool or a  shortbread tool for antibiotic  resistance genes  as well as serving as input into our  cgmlst tool set where we can  identify string typing in a  assembly free manner and actually um  add that if a new typing a new string  type has been identified  actually add that type to uh to a schema  database that you're currently using  and i haven't covered it here in this  presentation but  uh directly from this denoble assembly  uh step in this workflow you can also  see that we're identifying new genes  and finding prokaryotic genes in our  assemblies and  we're analyzing those genes with diamond  um and  providing a functional assessment and  classification of those genes  and then we're applying um an analysis  of pfam domains  and gene ontology or go annotations as  well be applied  and this enables researchers to take  into novel assembly  identify genes and then do a functional  classification  um on what sort of other um genomic  or genetic capabilities or  features are present in the in the  organism of interest  so today um as a brief review uh  we kind of overviewed some of the  capabilities of the clc genomics pro  suite  specifically really kind of went into  detail about the microbial genomics  module as it relates to strain typing  and strain characterization for amr  we learned how to download data from sra  use excel uh  as a starting point for importing  metadata and then assigning that  metadata to your actual samples in clc  we uh reviewed how to download mlst  schemas do general assemblies and strain  typing  as well as work with our antimicrobial  resistance  uh databases and analysis tools and  lastly and importantly  um one of the strengths of the clc  platform is the ability to build  single workflows that automate the  entire process from from one end to the  other  one other final comment i'll make here  um is  the underlying databases that we are  using throughout this  tutorial are customizable  so in pretty much every instance  users can download data directly from  genbank or  other public resources directly within  clc or if there are other databases that  you may have access to  or other public resources that have uh  draft assemblies or genomes that you  want to work with  um you can of course uh import those  into clc directly as well  um without using our integrated tools  but just using our standard importers to  create your own customized databases  and um this is this capability of  building  flexible microbial genomics databases is  really a strength of the platform  because  you can reapply this for microbiome  profiling on the fly  um you can also maintain and update your  own mlst schemas for isolates  et cetera there's a lot of applications  on the database  side and customization side that many  many folks find very useful  so for more information there's a link  here to our microbial genomics  portion of our research and discovery  portfolio  and uh thank you thank you very much for  your time  please feel free to contact us if you  have any further questions
 

